meta {
  name: Create Lead (Dash Row)
  type: http
  seq: 42
}

post {
  url: {{baseUrl}}/leads
  body: json
  auth: inherit
}

headers {
  Content-Type: application/json
}

body:json {
  {
    "createdDate": "2026-10-12",
    "primaryContact": {
      "firstName": "Avery",
      "lastName": "Quinn",
      "dateOfBirth": "1984-03-09",
      "phone": "(555) 299-9900",
      "email": "avery.quinn@example.com"
    },
    "address": {
      "street": "12 Quinn Court",
      "city": "Tulsa",
      "state": "OK",
      "zip": "74101"
    },
    "members": [],
    "leadSourceId": "{{reconLeadSourceId}}",
    "reconciliationRowId": "{{reconAssignRowId}}",
    "submissionToken": "{{$guid}}"
  }
}

script:post-response {
  if (res.getStatus() === 201) bru.setVar("reconLeadId", res.getBody().id);
}

tests {
  test("returns 201 Created", function () {
    expect(res.getStatus()).to.equal(201);
  });

  test("answers as any lead does: just its id", function () {
    expect(Object.keys(res.getBody())).to.deep.equal(["id"]);
  });
}

docs {
  # POST /leads with `reconciliationRowId` — step 1 of recording a Dash sale

  The New Lead form an assignee opens from **Record as sold** (or **Cancel
  rewrite**), seeded from `prefill`: the name Dash prints, and the lead dated
  the day Dash says the policy was written (`2026-10-12`), because a sale may
  not predate its lead. The endpoint is `Leads/Create Lead`; what
  `reconciliationRowId` adds (PAC-173 PR 4):

  - **No address dedupe.** The assignee always gets a fresh lead of their
    own, as for a `replacementIntent`: an open lead at the address could be a
    colleague's (the Sold form would 404 on it) or dated after the Dash sale.
    The submission-token and mailer signals still apply.
  - **The lead is stamped on the row** (`outcome.leadId`) after it is
    created, so resuming the outcome goes on to the Sold form with this lead
    rather than making another — only while the row is the caller's, with a
    Sold or Cancel rewrite at `record_sale` and no lead yet. On a cancel
    rewrite's replacement lead (`write_replacement`) nothing is stamped: the
    outcome keeps the sale's lead.
  - **Best-effort.** The lead is created and the response is the same
    `{ id }` whether or not the row could be stamped; nothing about the row
    ever fails this request.

  Every run of the folder books the Dash line again — `Review Row (Reopen
  Missing)` reopened it — so Avery Quinn's household collects a lead and a
  deal per run.
}
